8. AWS PLAN (later)
=====================================================================

Same repo, same code - mostly .env changes. The GitHub Actions workflow works for
EC2 too: change the secrets (host, user, key) and DEPLOY_PATH.

What maps to what:
- Server          -> EC2 (t3.micro / t4g.micro; new accounts since Jul 2025
                     get credits on a 6-month Free plan, not 12 months)
- MySQL           -> RDS MySQL or MySQL on the same EC2 at first
- Uploaded images -> S3 (+ CloudFront later)
- Email (OTP)     -> SES (needs "production access" request to email anyone)
- Domain / SSL    -> Route 53 + ACM (or Certbot on EC2)
- Secrets         -> .env on server first, later SSM Parameter Store
- Errors / logs   -> Bugsnag already set up, CloudWatch optional
- Set AWS billing alerts (Budgets) on day 1 so free tier never surprises.
- Never put AWS keys in the repo (private or not) - use IAM roles / GitHub OIDC.

Before moving to S3:
- Uploads use Storage::disk('public') hardcoded (FileUploadService,
  HomeSettingService, SystemSettingService, PageSettingService).
  Plan: point the 'public' disk to S3 in config/filesystems.php OR make the
  disk name come from .env. Copy storage/app/public/* to the S3 bucket.
- Change asset('storage/...') in views to Storage::url() so image URLs
  switch to S3 automatically.
- Behind an AWS load balancer: trustProxies (see To do).

Migration steps (Hostinger -> AWS):
- Build the AWS server while Hostinger stays live.
- mysqldump (--single-transaction --no-tablespaces, MySQL 8: --set-gtid-purged=OFF)
  -> import to RDS/EC2. A fresh database also works: migrate + the 3 seeders.
- Copy uploads to S3, update .env (DB_*, AWS_*, MAIL_* for SES, APP_URL).
- Update webhook URLs + Google OAuth redirect URIs if the domain changes.
- php artisan optimize, test checkout + OTP + image upload, then switch DNS.
- Cancel Hostinger hosting after a few quiet days; keep renewing the domain.

